 ## Overview
-Temporal Input Conditioning parameterizes scene attributes—such as position, rotation, opacity, density, or color—as explicit functions of time $t$ to enable novel view synthesis for dynamic scenes. It addresses the challenge of modeling non-rigid motion and temporal evolution from monocular or sparse multi-view inputs without requiring ground-truth geometry or dense multi-view stereo data at every instant. It sets itself apart from naive time-extended NeRFs by decomposing the representation into static canonical geometry and time-varying deformation fields, or by conditioning radiance fields directly on time, thereby exploiting temporal redundancy that direct 6D mappings fail to capture. Unlike [DaReNeRF](13782-darenerf.md), which focuses on direction-aware wavelet decompositions, this family establishes the foundational canonical-space and time-conditioning paradigms for dynamic neural rendering.
